# Working documents — kept on disk, not in the repo.
# One-off audits, session logs, research notes and the Ivy Document's 15MB of
# screenshots. They are useful locally and were noise in the history: 121 files
# nobody reads from GitHub. Untracked with `git rm --cached`, so every file is
# still sitting in docs/ on this machine.
#
# To bring one back: git add -f docs/THE_FILE.md
docs/

# ── Operational documents — on disk, never in the repo ──────────────────
# None of these hold a live credential (checked: no real keys, no real phone
# numbers, every .env.example value is a placeholder). They are out because of
# what they TELL somebody, not what they leak:
#
#   HUMAN_THREATS.md      The owner's anti-theft runbook. Section 2 is titled
#                         "What the system cannot catch" and names detection
#                         thresholds — a map for anyone wanting to steal.
#   DISASTER_RECOVERY.md  Backup schedule and restore procedure.
#   DEPLOY.md             Server and deployment topology.
#   PAYMENTS_DESIGN.md    The money path, end to end.
#   CLAUDE.md             Internal brief: the client's name, business state,
#                         and how I work. Goes nowhere near a repo that gets
#                         shown to another property.
#   .claude/              Working notes and session skills.
#
# To bring one back: git add -f THE_FILE.md
HUMAN_THREATS.md
DISASTER_RECOVERY.md
DEPLOY.md
PAYMENTS_DESIGN.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
